    "product_id": "6aac9811-a3a4-456e-98b9-8118a887f05c",
    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KPSR 141803\nAFDPSR\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Phoenix AZ\n1103 AM MST Sat Nov 14 2026\n\n.UPDATE...\nUpdated Aviation\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- A weak weather disturbance will move across northern Arizona today \nbringing additional chances for light showers.\n\n- Sunday and Monday will be warm and dry before another weather \nsystem brings rain chances and cooling temperatures back to the \nDesert Southwest Tuesday.\n\n- Another weather system could move through the region at the end of \nnext week that will help keep temperatures below normal. \n  \n&&\n\n.SHORT TERM /TODAY THROUGH MONDAY/... \nToday, a secondary shortwave will move through the region quickly \nmoving eastward traversing through northern AZ. This shortwave will \nbe weaker compared to yesterday's and as mentioned, positioned \nfurther north. As a result, isolated to scattered rain chances will \nbe confined to the higher terrain and foothills north and east of \nPhoenix. Best chances will be the early morning hours today before \nupper level ridging begins to fill in behind, bringing warmer and \ndrier conditions by tonight. PWATs will be around 0.6-0.8\" and there \nwill be little to no instability. Therefore, rainfall accumulations \nwill be light and generally around 0.10\" or less. Some wind gusts of \n20-30 mph are expected through the early morning hours today, \ngradually weakening over the course of the day as the shortwave \nmoves into the Four Corners region and continues eastwards.\n\nThis secondary shortwave will keep ample cloud cover overhead today, \nleading temperatures to cool even more. Highs today in the mid to \nupper 70s across SE CA and SW AZ, in the low 70s across south- \ncentral AZ, and in the 50s to mid 60s across the higher terrain \nareas. Behind this secondary shortwave upper level ridging will \nbuild over the Desert SW, leading to warmer and drier conditions \nstarting Sunday. H5 heights will climb back to between 579-582 dam \nresulting in temperatures to climb back into the upper 70s Sunday, \nand even back into the low 80s by Monday. By late Monday night \nanother system will be on the doorstep of the lower deserts, \nbringing another wave of cooler temperatures and better rain \nchances. \n\n&&\n\n.LONG TERM /TUESDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/... \nModels remain in good agreement that the next low pressure system \nwill reach the California coast early Monday and move into the lower \ndeserts by Tuesday morning. Tuesday rain chances will return to the \nforecast with NBM PoPs peaking between 50-80% in south- central AZ \nand the higher terrain areas, while SW AZ PoPs will be 20-40%. Rain \ntotals Tuesday are expected to be between 0.25\"-0.50\" to locally \nhigher (up to an inch) in the higher terrain. By Wednesday models \nare pointing towards yet again another low pressure system moving \ninland from the Pacific NW, however discrepancies between models are \nevident for how this system will track throughout the rest of the \nworkweek. Due to this confidence remains low for PoPs by mid to late \nnext week as things are too inconsistent at this time. \n\nDespite how the mid-week system tracks, a more significant cooling \ntrend can be counted on by the middle of the week as these series of \nweather systems move in from the northwest. They will likely usher \nin a cooler air mass with afternoon highs trending more towards the \nupper 60s to low 70s by late next week, with how much the \ntemperatures cool being dependent on the depth and position of the \nsystems. \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...Updated at 1803Z.\n\nSouth Central Arizona including KPHX, KIWA, KSDL, and KDVT:\nOccasional cigs in a 4-6K ft AGL will be the primary weather issue\nthrough Sunday morning. Confidence is good that light SE winds\nwill rapidly switch to W/SW early this afternoon, then revert back\nto east this evening. A period of light and variable winds is\nexpected prior to redeveloping the easterly component this\nevening. Lower cigs (4-6 kft) have developed late this morning and\nare expected to last through the afternoon. This evening, cigs\nshould lift aoa 6 kft. Some areas of SHRA have developed across\nthe Phoenix Metro so VCSH has been added to the KDVT, KSDL, and\nKPHX TAFs through 20-21Z. \n\nSoutheast California/Southwest Arizona including KIPL and KBLH:\nNo major weather issues will exist through the TAF period under\nbrief periods of cloud decks 5-7K ft AGL, and more FEW-SCT cirrus\nclouds through the afternoon. Confidence in wind direction\nforecasts are only moderate with variability common. In general,\nW/SW directions will be favored at KIPL, while a S/SW component\nwill be more common at KBLH. A period of enhanced winds, around 10\nkt with some occasional gusts in the upper teens, are expected at\nKIPL through the afternoon. Otherwise, wind speeds will be light\nand generally aob 6 kt. \n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER... \nMultiple weather systems are expected to move through the region \nover the next week bringing multiple chances for showers and a few \nstorms to the area. A secondary weather system will pass through \nNorthern Arizona today bringing rain chances mostly to the foothills \nand higher terrain areas north and east of Phoenix. In addition to \nthe rain chances, these systems will cool temperatures off below \nnormal. Dry conditions and warmer temperatures are then expected \nSunday and Monday before another weather system moves through the \nregion on Tuesday. A fourth weather system is then possible by the \nend of next week. These systems will bring additional rain chances \nto the region and cool temperatures back below normal. MinRHs will \nbe around 30-60% through next week, with excellent overnight \nrecovery. Occasional wind gusts of 20-30 mph will continue through \nthis morning, with winds returning to their light and diurnal \npatterns by this afternoon. Another period of gusty winds expected \nwith the weather system on Tuesday.\n\n&&\n\n.HYDROLOGY...\nGila: A Flood Warning remains in effect from Painted Rock Dam \nthrough the Dateland area through at least early next week due to \nwater released from the dam. Water release levels have now risen to \n3000 cfs and are expected to remain at this release level through \nthe next several days. This will have the potential to flood farm \nfields and low-water crossings along the river downstream from the \ndam.\n\n&&\n\n.PSR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nAZ...None.\nCA...None.\n&&\n\n$$\n\nSHORT TERM...Ryan/Berislavich\nLONG TERM...Ryan \nAVIATION...Berislavich\nFIRE WEATHER...Ryan/Berislavich \nHYDROLOGY...Lojero\n",
    "risk": "MEDIUM",
